  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to minimize the maximum overlap integral by decoupling the problem into the frequency domain, where spectral analysis reveals the root causes of correlations. The Erd\u0151s problem seeks to minimize the overlap between a function and its shifted complement, which is correlated with limiting the energy in positive frequencies while maintaining the mean energy constraint. Unlike previous methods that rely on local search or heuristic approaches, this strategy uses first-principles reductions to a convex quadratic program:\n\n1. Discretization and Fourier Transform: Represent the step function on n_points samples spaced uniformly on [0,2]. Apply a discrete Fourier transform to obtain frequency components with positive real parts.\n\n2. Penalized Optimization: Define an objective function in the frequency domain that discourages high peaks in the autocorrelation, leading to a smooth suppression of resonances.\n\n3. Resolution Independence: The spectral formulation automatically extends to arbitrary resolutions by controlling spectralshapescaling. Upsampling improves resolution without compromising feasibility.\n\n4. Nonlinear Projection: Bridges the domains between time (sampled) and frequency (analytic) representations, ensuring all intermediate candidates meet the integral constraint with high precision.\n\nThis method improves the parent solution by:\n- Greater exploration base through direct frequency control\n- Elimination of local minima that arise in time-domain optimization\n- Native handling of the approximation capabilities required by the problem\n- Clear correspondence to known constructions in harmonic analysis that have solved similar extremal problems\n\nMathematical specification\nLevels of abstraction in the optimization cascade:\n\n1. Temporal Domain:\n- Input: h(x) \u2208 [0,1] for x\u2208[0,2] with integral\u222bh(x)dx=1\n- Sampled form: Length n, dx=2/n, sum(h)_dx=1\n- Constraint: \u2200x, 0\u2264h(x)\u22641\n\n2. Spectral Transform:\n- Fourier coefficients: \u03be(\u03c9) = \u2211_{k=0}^{n-1} h[k] exp(-2\u03c0i\u03c9k/n)\n- Signal segmentation: Keep only components with \u03c9\u2264\u03c0/dx (Nyquist)\n\n3. Spectral Objective:\n- Smoothness regularization: E(\u03be) = \u2211_{\u03c9\u2264Nyquist} |\u03be(\u03c9)|\u00b2 exp(-\u03b3(fmin_\u03be(\u03c9)))\n- Penalized inversion: Minimize E(\u03be) subject to: \u222b|\u221af(\u03c9)d\u03c9| \u2248 1 + \u03b4\n\n4. Constructive Equivalence:\n- The optimal h is the inverse Fourier transform of a vector with entries truncated at the Nyquist boundary\n- The integral constraint is enforced by normalizing the spectrum's global energy after all transformations\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Parent Initialization:\n- Start with supplied parent (n=280 samples) if available, otherwise use piecewise linear interpolation\n- For missing initial values, generate a basic h_stem as:\n  - n_base = 100\n  - h = np.zeros(n)\n  - Insert piecewise linear pulses across [0,2] at resolution-dependent intervals\n\n2. Resolution Management:\n- Determine optimal resolution n = round(base * growth_factor)\n- Condition checks for overshoot: n \u2264 400\n- Linear interpolation for sample-to-sample up/downscaling:\n  - Preserve normalized integral via post-normalization\n\n3. Candidate Construction:\n- Base signal generation:\n  - h_base = np.zeros(n)\n  - Define event plates with width = dx/(4*k) for k=1,2,... to control temporal resolution\n  - Place events to minimize spectral energy beyond the Nyquist limit\n\n- Spectral shaping via penalized inversion in Ridgelet space:\n  - Transform h to spectral domain with fft\n  - Filter out frequencies above \u03c0/dx (standardizing to [0,2] domain)\n  - Apply iterative spectral concentration procedure:\n    h_spectral = optimal_qfilter(fsup=fs, fsia=secant_threshold)\n  - Forward transform back to time domain\n\n4. Optimization Cascade:\n- Gradient-based refinement with simulated annealing for:\n  max_iter_div = 2000, gain_factor=1.005\n- Evolution checks at interval=100:\n  Break if monotonically better for steplimit iterations\n- Summary statistics tracking for budget management\n\n5. Clipping and Repair:\n- Time-domain clamping: h = np.clip(h, 0,1)\n- Repair normalizer: delta = target_sum - np.sum(h)\n- Diophantine adjustment: 0.1% maximum structure modulation random walk to meet integral constraint\n\nSearch choices and useful variations\n1. Initial annealing strategy:\n- Single high-quality parent preferred over multiple low-quality trials\n- Use supplied parent if available, otherwise use piecewise linear stem (robust but not optimal)\n\n2. Critical parameters:\n- Spectral filter expense ratio: No fixed reciprocal cost bounds\n- Iteration greediness: Aggressive gain_factor=1.005 for emergent solutions\n- Diophantine repair probability: 1.0% (fixed by implementation)\n- Plateau-based early termination at improvement_rate < 1e-6 per day steps\n\n3. Compatibility fallbacks:\n- HasNotarent means use minimal setup from scratch each resolution\n- WorkWithPreimage capability preserves speedups with given parents\n- Level-specific repair automatically propagates constraints\n\n4. Adapters:\n- Temporal regularizer with offset adjust \u03b4=final_test_enable enables fallback to standard simulations\n- Fractional spectral cooling with neutral adjust epsilon=0.99 might help tricky cases\n\n5. Representation changes:\n- Normally time \u2192 spectrum \u2192 time\n- Optimal within-layer pen weeny offset might boost initial convergence\n\nComputebudget and robustness\nDominant costs:\n- Fourier transforms: O(n log n) per cascade level, with 280 samples being negligible\n- Scoring: negligible since compute_c5 is fast for modern machines\n- Number of levels: up to 12 (96 point capacity)\n\nSchedule:\n- Base run reward: 1100 seconds\n- Per-level budget: floor(5% of total time) default allocation\n- If compute_c5 becomes costly, employ level-specific budgetary caps\n\nFailsafes:\n- Early-break threshold: tolerate budget exhaustion in middle of spectrum relative to gains\n- Use nearest-neighbor repair if direct spectral invert times out\n- Preserve best state from last successful resolution if none found\n\nValidation and return contract\n1. Final candidate validation steps:\n- Check feasibility: assert np.min(h) >= clip_min and np.max(h) <= clip_max with clipping offset=0.001\n- Re-calculate integral with numerical precision checker\n- Re-compute c5 with a while-compatible snippet to confirm objective\n- Maintain at least three precision digits beyond the current record\n\n2. Required interface returns:\n- h_values: numpy array of length n_points\n- c5_bound: computed to two extra digits precision\n- n_points: latest successful resolution\n\nAdditional metadata:\n- Breakdown detail: total_time, cache_hits, repair_events\n\nReport format:\n[Method]: \"SpectralPenalty\"\n[Candidate]: {c5_bound}\n[Budget used]: [time taken]\n[Suitable]: True or False\n\nFall back validator:\n  h = [parent samples]\n  sum(h) = n/2.0 required by domain\n\nFor large domain inspections \u22652048 points, disabled by default with noted precision risks",
